## 1. What the iteration-4 fix got wrong / left incomplete

The iteration-4 commit (`73b5d52 test(v1.36.17): a ket probabilisztikus gate cseréje determinisztikusra`) touched exactly two test files — `tests/test_behavioral_typing.py` (log-normal AD gate → pinned-seed + calibration) and `tests/test_behavioral_simulation.py` (bezier gate → 25-seed ensemble). Verified from `git show --name-only 73b5d52`: no other test file changed. It left a **third probabilistic gate of the identical defect class** untouched:

- `tests/test_rate_limiter.py:240-252` — `test_uniform_distribution_ks_test`: draws 1000 **unseeded** samples and asserts `p > 0.05` from `scipy.stats.kstest`. By construction this rejects CORRECT code at α = 5%. This is the `1 failed` in the loop's own `1 failed, 2805 passed` full-suite result.
- `tests/test_rate_limiter.py:254-268` — `test_log_normal_distribution_ks_test`: same 5%-flake structure, **plus** it estimates `mean`/`std` from the same sample it tests (`:264-265`) before calling `kstest(..., "norm", args=(mean, std))` — the Lilliefors problem, so the p-value is invalid in both directions, not just flaky.
- `tests/test_rate_limiter.py:245` — the comment `# Temporarily override randomness for reproducibility` describes a pinning that **does not exist**: no seed is set anywhere in either test. A reader trusts the draw is reproducible; it is not.

So the concrete gap is `tests/test_rate_limiter.py:240-268` — two unseeded KS gates the fix never touched — compounded by the loop's own `analysis/next-moves.md:54` claim that "the other bisected flaky family is covered by the same fix," which this survivor refutes. (Caveat: my own 5 consecutive isolated runs of the two KS tests were all `2 passed`, and a 12-draw p-value probe never dipped below 0.11 — consistent with a ~5% flake, not proof of stability. The flake needs ~200 draws to manifest; see the loop's measured 10/200.)

## 2. DEFECT-001 (repo-root pytest) — ABOVE the line

**Above the line**: it must be fixed, because any bare-root verification command fails before measuring anything. Reproduced first-hand just now (read-only, `--collect-only` plus one full error-text run, `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, no tree writes):

- `$ .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q --collect-only -p no:cacheprovider -p no:randomly` → `Interrupted: 2 errors during collection` with the byte-exact cause:
  `import file mismatch: imported module 'test_rate_limiter' has this __file__ attribute: /home/zoltan/browser-helper/test_rate_limiter.py which is not the same as the test file we want to collect: /home/zoltan/browser-helper/tests/test_rate_limiter.py` (and the identical stanza for `test_proxy_pool_enhanced`).
- The same invocation's error section additionally surfaced live-environment failures (`test_parallel_session_isolation`, `test_mcp_integration`), confirming a root run is red for reasons unrelated to any suite state.
- The scoped command (`pytest tests/ -o addopts='' --no-header -q -p no:randomly --ignore=tests/test_parallel_session_isolation.py`, per the DEFECT-001 doc) is what the loop's `2805 passed` claim is true **of**.

Which command proves what: the **bare** command proves the repository cannot be verified from its root (collection aborts: `2 errors`); the **scoped** command proves only the state of that target+ignore subset. The loop's claim was accurate-but-narrow; anyone re-running bare `pytest` gets a red tree for a pre-existing structural reason (`29da9ee`, 2026-08-14 duplicates). Corroborating detail: the duplicate pairs are not even identical — `cmp test_rate_limiter.py tests/test_rate_limiter.py` differs at byte 17860/line 404, while the proxy pair is byte-identical — so "delete the root duplicates" needs a diff check first, not a blind `rm`. Also confirmed: no `testpaths` in `pyproject.toml:32-39`, no `tests/__init__.py` — neither durable fix is in place.

