## 5. What the reader would get wrong from my report alone — signals

- **"5× green KS runs" ≠ stable.** My isolated passes (5× `2 passed`, 12 p-values ≥ 0.11) are exactly what a 5%-flake looks like at small n. Do not downgrade item 1 on their strength; the loop's 10/200 (= 5.0% = kstest α) is the correctly-powered measurement.
- **Exit-code illusion on the bare run.** One of my bare invocations piped through `tail` surfaced `EXIT=0` from the pipe, not from pytest — the ground truth is the `Interrupted: 2 errors during collection` banner and the mismatch stanzas, not any trailing echo.
- **"Duplicates" ≠ identical.** Only the proxy pair is byte-identical; the rate-limiter pair diverged (line 404+), so the fix needs a content decision per file, and `cmp` output above is the check.
- **The verdict store is no longer empty.** The post-task review (`:111-113`) truthfully reported zero recorded verdicts at its measurement time; `v20261005134000-741226` exists now. A reader of the review alone would conclude PART 4b is still unused — it has since been used once, for this verdict.
- **"IN PROGRESS" sections look deliberate.** The stale `:58-71` blocks carry `(eredeti …)` / Hungarian annotations that read like intentional history. They are history, but unlike `:20` they lack the SUPERSEDED marker — treat the missing marker, not the content, as the defect.
- **NOT re-measured (honest gaps):** full-suite `1 failed, 2805 passed` was not re-run (out of scope for a read-only review and unnecessary — the failing node is named and its class established); the live `/type` check from the review was not repeated; no mutation probe was run against the KS gates.
